## Checked and rejected

The study also claimed our skill-path documentation is broken (`.agents/skills/...` not resolving). **Verified against main and rejected** — the live references resolve; the single broken pointer is in `docs/plans/archive/google-signup-child-app-plan.md`, an archived plan. Not worth fixing.

## Do not regress these

The same study found our DX already ahead in ways worth protecting:

- **Local codebase access is genuinely built in** — `read`/`grep`/`find`/`bash`, workspace rooting, UI and HTTP. Flue required explicitly mounting a host sandbox.
- **HTTP needs no application files.** Flue needed Hono, Vite, `@flue/vite`, a router and a Vite config.
- **Tool + skill hot reload in one trust-scoped package**, with the skill found without dependency installation.
- **The verifier is candid about its own limits** — improve coverage without losing that explicitness.
- **Stable HTTP error envelopes** (`SESSION_NOT_FOUND` etc.) where Flue returned a raw stack.
- **Credentials are not copied into every project** — Pi's auth store serves several providers without a per-project `.env`.
- **The plugin docs' glossary and trust model** are the clearest of the three about runtime vs app/internal. Simplify the local path without erasing that distinction.
## The organising principle: smart defaults, visible, fully overridable

The nine items above are individually worth doing, but they share one stance worth stating explicitly, because it decides how each is implemented.

**Show the wow first. Never ask a question you can answer yourself. Print every choice you made. Make overriding it obvious.**

### Two journeys, only one of which is nearly there

**Journey 1 — use it.** `npx @hachej/boring-ui-cli` in any folder already opens a workspace with codebase tools, a UI and HTTP. That is genuinely closer to a wow than either Flue or eve: 0.283s scaffold, 41.5s to verified discovery, no application files needed for HTTP.

**One thing destroys it.** From `packages/cli/README.md`:

> "The CLI does not take an API key flag. On first run, if no LLM provider is configured it prints a guide: in another terminal run `pi` (or `npx @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`) and use `/login` … refresh the browser afterward."

The first act of our product is to send a developer to install and authenticate a **different CLI, in a second terminal**, then come back and refresh. Nothing else on this list costs us more first impressions.

**Fix:** accept a credential inline — `--api-key`, an env var, or one in-place interactive prompt — and write it to the same `~/.pi/agent/auth.json` pi already owns. Keep pi's auth store as the backend; just stop making the user leave to populate it. Subscription sign-in can still hand off, but a plain API key must never require a second tool.

**Journey 2 — extend it.** This is where the six required concepts live, and where the ranked items apply.

### Four layers, each opt-in, none a prerequisite

| Layer | What it is | Who touches it |
|---|---|---|
| **L0 — zero config** | `npx boring-ui` in a folder. Host, workspace root, sandbox mode, plugin tier, session root all detected. One credential prompt if none is found. | everyone, always |
| **L1 — flags** | `--port`, `--host`, `--agent-only`, `--sandbox direct\|bwrap`, `--api-key` | the 20% who need one thing changed |
| **L2 — config file** | the same choices, persisted per project | teams standardising |
| **L3 — programmatic** | today's full composition: app/internal plugins, `extraTools`, static fleet, trusted host capabilities | app authors and us |

The rule that makes this real: **L0 must never be a dead end.** Today, moving from CLI to customisation means understanding the whole architecture at once. Each layer should expose exactly the next one — `--sandbox` teaches that sandbox mode exists; the config file teaches what else is settable; the programmatic API is reached only when a flag genuinely cannot express the need.

### Defaults must be visible, not invisible

This is what gets us discoverability without asking a question. On startup, print one line of resolved choices:

```
workspace  /home/j/projects/acme  (git root)
sandbox    bwrap                  (detected; --sandbox direct to change)
plugins    runtime tier           (no trusted routes requested)
sessions   ~/.pi/agent/sessions
model      anthropic/claude-…     (from ~/.pi/agent/auth.json)
```

Five lines that replace six decisions. The developer learns the axes exist, sees what was chosen, and knows the flag to change it — without being blocked before anything runs. This is also why item 9 (collapse the plugin-shape decision) should *print* the tier it selected rather than silently choosing.

### The defaults we should commit to

| Decision | Default | Escape |
|---|---|---|
| host shape | CLI folder mode | `workspaces`, or the programmatic hosts |
| workspace root | git root of cwd, else cwd | positional arg |
| sandbox | bwrap if available, else direct **with a printed notice** | `--sandbox` |
| plugin tier | runtime/generated | requesting a trusted route selects app/internal |
| tool surface | Pi resource (hot-reloadable) | `extraTools` at L3 |
| credentials | existing pi auth store, else one inline prompt | `--api-key`, env var, or `pi /login` for subscriptions |
| session root | `~/.pi/agent/sessions`, checked writable at startup | `BORING_AGENT_SESSION_ROOT` |

Every row: a default that works, and an escape that does not require understanding the other six.

### Why we can afford this

The integrated runtime is already the differentiator — Flue needed Hono, Vite, a router and a Vite config for HTTP, and an explicit host sandbox mount for codebase access. We have all of it built in. The wow is already built; it is currently gated behind questions and a second terminal.
